Subject: =?utf-8?q?Re=3A_=5BRqtl-disc=5D_A_weird_LOD_peak_when_using_=E2=80=9Chk?=
 =?utf-8?q?=E2=80=9D_method?=
Message-ID: <CSR4UeYGWy8.bNkFpGQFBQAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <CSR4UeYGWy8.6TDdwxr1BAAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <CSR4UeYGWy8.6TDdwxr1BAAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/CSR4UeYGWy8/m/bNkFpGQFBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There are a very small proportion of heterozygous genotypes, but when read into R/qtl, they are being treated as an F2 intercross. Haley-Knott doesn't work well in this case.

I think it's best to omit the heterozygous genotypes and treat it as RIL by selfing, in which case the peak from Haley-Knott regression goes away.

Either include

   crosstype="riself"

in the call to read.cross(), or use

   data <- convert2riself(data)

after having read the data into R.

karl

